<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matthew C. Perry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1794">1794</a>–<a href="/years/1858">1858</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Commodore Matthew Perry, Perry</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matthew C. Perry was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> naval officer who led an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1853 and 1854. Backed by modern warships, he forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> to open Japanese ports through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-kanagawa">Convention of Kanagawa</a>. The episode exposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s military weakness and contributed to its downfall.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry.jpg" alt="Matthew C. Perry" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathew Benjamin Brady · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Commodore_Matthew_Calbraith_Perry.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century Western ships arrived: French missionaries were landed in 1844, and <b>Commodore Matthew Perry</b> used Naha as a base in 1853–1854 and signed a compact with the kingdom in 1854, followed by agreements with France in 1855 and the Netherlands in 1859.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The shogunate's military weakness was exposed in 1853 when <b>Commodore Matthew Perry</b>'s 'Black Ships', a squadron that included steam-powered warships armed with modern guns, entered Edo Bay and demanded that Japan open its ports to American ships.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Commodore <b>Matthew C. Perry</b>’s American expedition reached Japan in 1853 and returned with large warships to conclude the Convention of Kanagawa in 1854, which opened Shimoda and Hakodate.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>It has been recorded by singers from Luis Miguel, Rocío Dúrcal, Miguel Aceves Mejía and Lila Downs to <b>Perry</b> Como, Nana Mouskouri, Julio Iglesias, Hibari Misora and Joan Baez, and Franco Battiato took up its refrain in his own song &quot;Cuccurucucù&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cucurrucucu-paloma">Cucurrucucú paloma</a></li><li><blockquote>US <b>Commodore Matthew Perry</b> anchored four warships off Uraga and demanded that Japan open its ports.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-8">8 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Stephen <b>Perry</b> of the London firm Messrs Perry and Co patented bands made of vulcanised rubber for holding papers together.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-17">17 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
